  q(GOV,'A household is headed by one parent raising children without a partner. What type of family is this?','A single-parent family',['An extended family','A nuclear family','A blended family'],'A','Single-parent families are common and may face added financial and time pressures.',KATO),
  q(GOV,'Which program gives temporary cash assistance and job support to low-income families with children?','TANF (Temporary Assistance for Needy Families)',['WIC (Women, Infants and Children)','IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act)','FERPA (Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act)'],'B','TANF is funded by federal block grants and run by the states, with work requirements and time limits.',POL),
  q(GOV,'Which Medicaid benefit gives children regular checkups, screenings and treatment?','EPSDT (Early and Periodic Screening, Diagnostic and Treatment)',['Medicare Part B outpatient coverage for adults','The Older Americans Act meal programs','Social Security disability income payments'],'C','EPSDT covers well-child visits, vision, hearing and dental screenings and needed treatment for children on Medicaid.',POL),
  q(GOV,'A child learns the customs, language and values of their own culture while growing up. What is this process called?','Enculturation',['Assimilation','Ethnocentrism','Acculturation'],'D','Enculturation is learning one’s own culture; acculturation is adapting to a new culture.',KATO),
  q(GOV,'A new immigrant feels confused and anxious when facing unfamiliar customs and language. What is this called?','Culture shock',['Cultural humility','Ethnocentrism','Stereotyping'],'A','Culture shock usually eases with time, support and learning about the new culture.',KATO),
  q(GOV,'Grandparents raise their grandchildren because the parents cannot. What type of arrangement is this?','Kinship care',['Foster care with strangers','Assisted living','Respite care'],'B','Kinship care keeps children with relatives and often reduces the trauma of separation.',KATO),
  q(GOV,'Research on divorce suggests which factor most harms children’s adjustment?','Ongoing high conflict between parents',['Living in two different homes','Having different bedtimes at each home','Having a stepparent who is kind'],'C','Children generally adjust better when parents cooperate; continued conflict is linked to more problems.',KATO),
  q(GOV,'A nurse visits first-time, low-income mothers at home during pregnancy and the child’s first two years. What type of program is this?','A home visiting program',['A hospice program','A Medicare Advantage plan','A school lunch program'],'D','Programs such as Nurse-Family Partnership improve prenatal health, child development and family stability.',POL),
  q(GOV,'A society expects boys to be tough and girls to be nurturing. What are these expectations called?','Gender roles',['Genetic traits','Developmental milestones','Reflexes'],'A','Gender roles are learned behaviors and expectations that vary across cultures and over time.',KATO),
  q(GOV,'Who usually sets the vaccine requirements children must meet to enter school?','State laws',['Each classroom teacher','The child’s pediatrician only','The Federal Reserve'],'B','States set school immunization requirements and the rules for any medical or other exemptions.',POL),
  q(THEO,'In Freud’s theory, which part of the personality is present at birth and seeks immediate pleasure?','Id',['Ego','Superego','Conscience'],'C','The id follows the pleasure principle; the ego develops later to deal with reality.',KATO),
  q(THEO,'In Freud’s theory, which stage occurs at about ages 3 to 6, when children notice differences between boys and girls?','Phallic stage',['Oral stage','Anal stage','Latency stage'],'D','Freud believed children identify with the same-sex parent during the phallic stage.',KATO),
  q(THEO,'Which theorist described the zone of proximal development?','Lev Vygotsky',['Jean Piaget','Sigmund Freud','Erik Erikson'],'A','The zone is the gap between what a learner can do alone and what they can do with help from others.',DHO),
  q(THEO,'A child cleans their room so their parent will stop nagging. Which operant conditioning concept is this?','Negative reinforcement',['Positive punishment','Classical conditioning','Extinction'],'B','Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant to increase a behavior; it is not punishment.',KATO),
  q(THEO,'Konrad Lorenz found that baby geese followed the first moving object they saw. What concept does this show?','Imprinting during a critical period',['Conservation of volume','Theory of mind','Scaffolding by adults'],'C','Some behaviors develop only during a specific window early in life.',KATO),
  q(THEO,'A teen has explored different options and made firm commitments to values and goals. Which identity status is this?','Identity achievement',['Identity diffusion','Identity foreclosure','Moratorium'],'D','Marcia’s statuses are based on whether a person has explored options and made commitments.',KATO),
  q(THEO,'Which theorist believed development mainly unfolds through a biological timetable of maturation?','Arnold Gesell',['Albert Bandura','B. F. Skinner','Lev Vygotsky'],'A','Gesell created age-based developmental norms; Bandura and Skinner emphasized learning.',KATO),
  q(THEO,'A preschooler talks out loud to themself while solving a puzzle. What did Vygotsky call this?','Private speech',['Egocentric thinking','Animism','Telegraphic speech'],'B','Vygotsky saw private speech as a tool that becomes silent inner thought.',KATO),
  q(PRE,'When a newborn is held upright with the feet touching a surface, it makes walking movements. Which reflex is this?','Stepping reflex',['Rooting reflex','Moro reflex','Palmar grasp reflex'],'C','The stepping reflex usually disappears by about 2 months and returns later as voluntary walking.',POL),
  q(PRE,'What is the single cell formed when a sperm fertilizes an egg?','A zygote',['An embryo','A fetus','A placenta'],'D','The zygote divides and implants during the germinal period of the first two weeks.',POL),
  q(PRE,'An Rh-negative mother carries an Rh-positive baby. Why is she given Rh immune globulin?','To keep her from making antibodies that could harm future babies',['To increase the baby’s birth weight before delivery','To prevent morning sickness in the first trimester of pregnancy','To speed up labor and delivery'],'A','Rh immune globulin prevents sensitization, which could cause hemolytic disease in later pregnancies.',POL),
  q(PRE,'At about what age do infants begin to smile in response to people (a social smile)?','About 6 to 8 weeks',['About 1 week','About 6 months','About 1 year'],'B','The social smile is an important early milestone in social development.',POL),
  q(PRE,'At about what distance can a newborn see most clearly?','About 8 to 12 inches',['About 2 inches','About 3 feet','About 20 feet'],'C','This is about the distance to a caregiver’s face during feeding.',POL),
  q(PRE,'A newborn weighing less than about how much is considered low birth weight?','5.5 pounds (2,500 grams)',['3 pounds (1,360 grams)','7.5 pounds (3,400 grams)','9 pounds (4,080 grams)'],'D','Low birth weight increases the risk of breathing, feeding and developmental problems.',POL),
  q(CHILD,'A child watches others play and talks to them but does not join in. What type of play is this?','Onlooker play',['Cooperative play','Associative play','Solitary play'],'A','Onlooker play is one of Parten’s stages of social play, between solitary and parallel play.',KATO),
  q(CHILD,'A 3-year-old repeats sounds and words while excited to speak. How is this usually viewed?','Normal disfluency that often resolves as language grows',['A sign of a serious hearing loss in all cases','A sign that the child needs speech punishment','A sign of intellectual disability'],'B','Many preschoolers go through a period of disfluency; persistent or severe stuttering should be evaluated.',KATO),
  q(CHILD,'A 13-year-old soccer player has a concussion. What is the usual recommendation?','Brief rest, then a gradual return to school and sports once symptom-free',['Return to the same game if the headache is mild','Several weeks of complete darkness and no activity at all','Return to full contact the next day to rebuild confidence'],'C','The developing brain is vulnerable; a stepwise return-to-play plan under medical guidance reduces the risk of a second injury.',KATO),
  q(CHILD,'A preschooler wakes screaming but cannot be comforted and remembers nothing the next day. What is most likely?','A night terror',['A nightmare','Sleepwalking','Sleep apnea'],'D','Night terrors happen during deep sleep; nightmares occur during REM sleep and are usually remembered.',POL),
  q(CHILD,'How does the timing of the adolescent growth spurt differ between boys and girls?','Boys usually have it about 2 years later than girls',['Boys usually have it about 5 years earlier than girls','Boys and girls always have it at the same age','Only girls have an adolescent growth spurt'],'A','Girls’ growth spurt usually begins around 10 to 11; boys’ begins around 12 to 13.',POL),
  q(CHILD,'Which is one of the leading causes of death among U.S. teenagers?','Suicide',['Alzheimer disease','Osteoporosis','Prostate cancer'],'B','Suicide is a leading cause of teen death; warning signs include withdrawal and talk of hopelessness.',POL),
  q(CHILD,'A student is repeatedly harassed and threatened through social media posts and texts. What is this called?','Cyberbullying',['Parallel play','Social comparison','Identity foreclosure'],'C','Cyberbullying can be constant and public; it is linked to anxiety, depression and school avoidance.',KATO),
  q(CHILD,'By about what age do children label themselves as boys or girls?','About 2 to 3 years',['About 6 months','About 8 to 9 years','About 13 to 14 years'],'D','Gender identity labeling appears early; gender constancy develops by about 6 to 7 years.',KATO),
  q(CHILD,'A child of average intelligence has persistent trouble decoding words and reading. Which learning disability is likely?','Dyslexia',['Dyscalculia','Dysgraphia','Aphasia'],'A','Dyslexia affects reading; early support with structured reading programs helps.',KATO),
  q(CHILD,'A teen’s partner checks their phone, controls who they see and threatens them. What is this?','Dating violence',['Normal romantic jealousy','Social learning','Parallel play'],'B','Controlling and threatening behavior is abuse; teens should know where to get help.',POL),
  q(CHILD,'At what stage is the pressure to conform to friends usually strongest?','Early to middle adolescence',['Infancy','Early childhood','Late adulthood'],'C','Peer conformity peaks in early adolescence and declines as identity becomes stronger.',KATO),
  q(ADULT,'An adult accepts that problems can have more than one correct answer depending on the situation. What is this type of thinking called?','Postformal thought',['Concrete operational thought','Preoperational thought','Sensorimotor thought'],'D','Postformal thought goes beyond Piaget’s stages to include flexible, practical reasoning.',KATO),
  q(ADULT,'At what age is binge drinking most common?','Ages 18 to 25',['Ages 45 to 55','Ages 65 to 75','Ages 80 and older'],'A','Early adults have the highest rates of binge drinking, which raises the risk of injury and alcohol use disorder.',POL),
  q(ADULT,'At about what age does female fertility begin to decline more rapidly?','In the mid-30s',['In the early teens','In the early 20s','In the late 50s'],'B','Egg quantity and quality fall with age, especially after about 35.',POL),
  q(ADULT,'A person is competitive, impatient and always in a hurry. Which personality pattern is this?','Type A personality',['Type B personality','Introverted personality','Slow-to-warm-up temperament'],'C','Type A behavior, especially hostility, has been linked to higher heart disease risk.',POL),
  q(ADULT,'About how much sleep do most adults need each night?','About 7 to 9 hours',['About 3 to 4 hours','About 4 to 5 hours','About 11 to 12 hours'],'D','Too little sleep is linked to obesity, heart disease, depression and accidents.',POL),
  q(ADULT,'In Levinson’s theory, what is “the Dream” that young adults form?','A vision of the life they hope to achieve',['A memory from early childhood','A sleep disorder in adulthood','A plan for retirement only'],'A','Levinson believed young adults form a Dream and may revise it during the midlife transition.',KATO),
  q(ADULT,'In Sternberg’s theory, a relationship with intimacy, passion and commitment is which type of love?','Consummate love',['Romantic love','Companionate love','Empty love'],'B','Consummate love includes all three components; companionate love has intimacy and commitment.',KATO),
  q(GERI,'Which theory says aging results from damage caused by unstable oxygen molecules in cells?','Free radical theory',['Activity theory','Continuity theory','Disengagement theory'],'C','Free radicals damage cell parts over time; antioxidants may help limit this damage.',KATO),
  q(GERI,'An older adult has joint pain and stiffness from cartilage breaking down over time. What is this?','Osteoarthritis',['Rheumatoid arthritis','Gout','Osteoporosis'],'D','Osteoarthritis is the most common joint disease in older adults; activity and weight control help.',POL),
  q(GERI,'Which is a warning sign of financial abuse of an older adult?','Sudden changes in bank accounts or new names added to them',['Regular visits from family members','Using a weekly pill organizer','Joining a senior center'],'A','Financial abuse is common; health workers should report suspected elder abuse.',POL),
  q(GERI,'After death, the body gradually cools to room temperature. What is this called?','Algor mortis',['Rigor mortis','Livor mortis','Cheyne-Stokes'],'B','Algor mortis is the cooling of the body; rigor mortis is stiffening.',DHO),
  q(GERI,'A staff member uses old photos and music to help residents talk about pleasant memories. What is this activity called?','Reminiscence therapy',['Reality orientation for delirium','Physical therapy','Occupational licensing'],'C','Reminiscence therapy can improve mood and social connection in older adults.',POL),
  q(GERI,'What is the difference between gerontology and geriatrics?','Gerontology studies aging; geriatrics is medical care of older adults',['Gerontology treats children; geriatrics treats teens','They mean exactly the same thing','Geriatrics studies teeth; gerontology studies bones'],'D','Gerontology covers the biological, psychological and social study of aging.',POL),
  q(GERI,'Which group of older adults is often called the “old-old” or “oldest-old”?','People 85 and older',['People 50 to 55','People 60 to 64','People 65 to 69'],'A','The 85+ group is the fastest-growing age group and often needs the most care.',KATO),
  q(GERI,'Which signs often show that death is approaching?','Mottled skin and decreasing urine output',['Rising energy and appetite','Bright pink skin and fast healing','Increased thirst and urination'],'B','As circulation slows, the skin becomes cool and mottled and urine output falls.',DHO)
